EddingtonBias — Eddington Bias: Noise Creates Massive Galaxies
============================================

Group: Astrostatistics

Open index.html directly, or serve it with TeachingTools alongside this
directory. The Canvas lesson runs entirely in JavaScript without Shiny, WebR,
external downloads or data upload. Original app.R and model.R are unchanged.

Model
-----
8,000 true log10 masses follow the NORMALISED truncated exponential
  f(x) = k exp[-k(x-8.5)] / [1-exp(-3.5k)], 8.5 <= x <= 12.
k is a natural-exponential rate per dex, not a base-10 power-law exponent.
Observed log mass is x + sigma*z, with independent standard Normal z.
The true and measured curves use the exact finite-boundary convolution:
  k/Z exp[k(L-y)+(k*sigma)^2/2]
    * [Phi((U-y+k*sigma^2)/sigma)-Phi((L-y+k*sigma^2)/sigma)].
At zero noise the analytic density is the true truncated density.

Up/down metrics count crossings, not signs of individual errors. The complete
2x2 migration table verifies measured high = true high + up - down and total
count = 8,000. Measurements outside the true [8.5,12] bounds remain included.
The lesson never claims that every finite realisation has positive net bias.

All original controls, presets, guides and explanations are retained. The
60-step animation scales cached Gaussian draws from zero to the selected SD.
Threshold changes only reclassify the same observations; noise changes retain
the true masses. Slope changes transform the same underlying uniform draws.

Intentional differences / R limitations
--------------------------------------
Astro.createRng(seed) is reproducible but differs from R's RNG. Both histograms
are shown, with common 0.1-dex bins covering every simulated value for the whole
animation. Unlike the R fixed plotting window, no simulated tail is hidden.
Density normalisation always refers to the full population, not the visible
window. All histogram counts are retained; excluded is zero in this lesson.

The R expression subtracts positive-tail CDF values that can both round to
one, losing tiny but resolvable densities. The port uses the equivalent
reflected-tail difference and log-domain prefactor, without changing the
underlying model. This numerical difference is explained in the page.

Numerical API / validation
-------------------------
CommonJS require("./model.js") / browser EddingtonBiasModel exports defaults,
massDensity(x, slope, noise=0, lower=8.5, upper=12), compute(parameters), and
atPhase(result, phase). compute returns true masses, cached standard Normal
errors, shared bin edges, the true histogram and the final measured summary.
atPhase returns measurements, histogram, migration counts and noise SD.
Pure Node calls validate input domains using Astro.ValidationError.

Run from the repository root with Node 22+:
  node --test EddingtonBias/test_model.js
  TMPDIR="$PWD/EddingtonBias" node EddingtonBias/test_browser.js

Tests check independently R-calibrated density values, unit-area quadrature
including tails, the zero-error limit, a positive-tail cancellation case,
fixed random draws, count conservation at intermediate frames, all control
boundaries, and invalid inputs. Browser tests add plot-colour checks, zero
scatter, threshold invariance and migration bookkeeping; the shared suite
covers presets/reset, playback, errors, keyboard tabs, mobile and file://.

Runtime assets (no others)
-------------------------
index.html, model.js, app.js, ../TeachingTools/statistics.js,
../TeachingTools/browser.js, ../TeachingTools/browser.css,
../TeachingTools/lesson.js.
